 Outline rev 5: answers review 2 (disclosure in the note's words, abstract rows, lead on counts/null/prediction, calibration plot, compact Table 5 in main)\n9bef4c9 Outline rev 4: decision rules cite PREANALYSIS rev 2 instead of copying margins\ndiff --git a/Outline.md b/Outline.md\nindex b203c22..4d78a0e 100644\n--- a/Outline.md\n+++ b/Outline.md\n@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@\n-# Outline (revision 5, answers review 2)\n+# Outline (revision 6, answers review 2 and figtab's figure decisions)\n \n Works from the `Brief` of `idea-repo` (wiki revision 6; the corrected noise curve and \"Q ≥ 1 only in the linear regime\" are in since its revision 4). The outline says what to test and what each part of the paper must say; it does not say what the result is. Every claim below has the outcome that would make it false and the sentence the paper then writes instead.\n \n@@ -99,7 +99,7 @@ Each main figure carries one claim; `figtab-repo` decides how it is drawn, and t\n \n | Item | Says | Needs from analysis |\n |---|---|---|\n-| **Fig. 1 (teaser)** | Panel A, the calibration plot: observed k\\* against the k\\* predicted by the same-graph null without a search (log–log, one point or binned median per z-stratum and dataset, per design), with the equivalence margin drawn as a ratio band exp(±δ) around the diagonal. Panel B: Q_real/Q_null against z (per design, uncertainty, same band): where designs leave the common scale. Panel B alone would be a flat line near 1 and is not the figure. Equal-N subsampling (observed k\\*/N against its expected N^(−1/2) trend, F/z against N) is panel C, or the second panel if the page budget demands, in place of the former Fig. 2. (C1, C4, C5) | k\\*, z, N per conclusion, null k\\* (predicted and sampled), labelled by design, CI; subsampled-N curves |\n+| **Fig. 1 (teaser)** | Panel A, the calibration plot: observed k\\* against the k\\* predicted by the same-graph null without a search (log–log, one point or binned median per z-stratum and dataset, per design), with the equivalence margin drawn as a ratio band exp(±δ) around the diagonal. Panel B: Q_real/Q_null against z (per design, uncertainty, same band): where designs leave the common scale. Panel B alone would be a flat line near 1 and is not the figure. Two panels (5.5 in wide; three would be 1.8 in each). The equal-N subsampling (observed k\\*/N against its expected N^(−1/2) trend, F/z against N) is its own **Fig. 2** (as `figtab-repo` drew it), so `paper-repo` can move it to the appendix if pages run out. The grey band is a visual guide of the size of the Δ margin on k\\*, not the decision rule (which applies to the interval of Δ on log Q); the caption says so. (C1, C4, C5) | k\\*, z, N per conclusion, null k\\* (predicted and sampled), labelled by design, CI; subsampled-N curves |\n | **Fig. 3** | Two panels. Left: the fraction of CI-separated pairs (and top-10 relations) surviving b alterations, b in absolute count with %-of-N on a second axis, with the P_flip(f) / bootstrap noise curve as the reference. Right: the same in SE units, the cross-dataset axis (C2, C3) | survival curves, P_flip curves, CI |\n | **Fig. 4** | Real − Null Q per matched contrast (Arena crowd vs each judge, MT-Bench human vs GPT-4, NBA seasons as replicates) with uncertainty; paired differences (C4) | matched Q with joint cluster bootstrap |\n | **Fig. 5 (appendix or main if room)** | Search quality: first-order vs exact, lower bound and upper bound (C6) | exact/LB per anchor |\n@@ -111,7 +111,7 @@ Each main figure carries one claim; `figtab-repo` decides how it is drawn, and t\n \n Table 2 and Fig. 1 are the paper's main result and are built first.\n \n-**Main body vs appendix, decided now** so the page limit does not shape results later. Main: Table 1 (compact), Table 2, Figs. 1, 3, 4 (the former Fig. 2 is a panel of Fig. 1; the numbering of Figs. 3–4 is kept to avoid confusing the neighbors), the **compact Tab\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "720ad9e8-5b59-5cec